{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>His eyes are darker than wine.</b> This too is a metaphor for the abundance of wine; he says that each and every one in the land of Judah will have eyes darkened from wine. The word is inverted, its form being כחלילי, and the meaning is that just as a person darkens his eyes with antimony, so in the land of Judah they will darken them with wine. <b>And white of teeth from milk.</b> A metaphor too for the abundance of milk." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> חכלילי — a palate whose taste says to me, 'to me, to me.' <b>And know the view of Onkelos</b> in the verse: the mountains in the land of Judah grow red with vineyards, and therefore he likened them to eyes, which stand at the height of the body — so too the mountains are high above the earth. And he said that the valleys grow white with grain and with flocks of sheep from which comes the milk; the Targum of 'teeth' (שנים) is from the language of 'teeth of rocks' (שיני הסלעים). And if you look at this passage, in the blessing of Judah, you will find there all the letters of the Torah inscribed except for the letter zayin. And the reason is that the kingdom of Israel, which comes from Judah — their essence of victory is not through weapons (כלי זיין) as with other nations, for the sword is the inheritance of Esau; but the kingdom of Israel does not inherit the land by the sword and does not operate according to the way of nature and the strength of the hand, but only according to merit and punishment, by the power of the Most High, blessed be He. And from this you will find in the name of Judah — from whom comes the kingdom — that the special Name (השם המיוחד) is complete; for when Israel do the will of the Place (המקום), Israel performs might and their kingdom grows strong and succeeds, and the special Name is with them — they have no need for weapons (כלי זיין). Therefore the letter zayin was missing from the blessing of Judah. And the reason that the letter dalet was added to the letters of the Name in his name is because he was fourth in birth, or because the sun in the sphere is fourth and was created on the fourth day, and the kingdom of Judah was likened to the sun, as it says (Psalms 89): ״וכסאו כשמש נגדי״. Or perhaps one may say further that since the attribute of Judah is called זאת, and it is crowned with the crown of zayin, therefore there was no need to mention it — for he already mentioned it in what he said, ״ידך בעורף אויבך״, which is the bow, about which it is written (Genesis 9:12): ״זאת אות הברית״, and this is what is said (II Samuel 1:18): ״ללמד בני יהודה קשת הלא היא כתובה על ספר הישר״, and Moses, peace be upon him, came and explained: ״וזאת ליהודה״, and similarly he mentioned there (Deuteronomy 33:7): ״ורב לו״." "<b>And further.</b> We may say another reason why the letter zayin was missing from his name: because Judah himself is seventh in souls among the patriarchs — Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, Reuben, Simeon, Levi, and Judah. If so, Judah corresponds to the seventh day, the Sabbath. And therefore he mentioned in him ״יודוך אחיך״, for so you find regarding the Sabbath (Psalms 92:2): ״טוב להודות לה׳״. And he mentioned ״מטרף בני עלית״ from the language of (Proverbs 30:8): ״הטריפני לחם חקי״, and the intention is to say that on the Sabbath you must withdraw yourself even from the preparation of food, as it is written (Deuteronomy 5:14): ״לא תעשה כל מלאכה״. And he said ״כרע רבץ כאריה וכלביא מי יקימנו״, just as is written regarding the Sabbath (Exodus 16:29): ״שבו איש תחתיו״. And ״מחוקק מבין רגליו״ alludes to circumcision, which overrides the Sabbath. And he said ״אסרי לגפן עירה ולשורקה בני אתונו״, for so it is written regarding the Sabbath (Exodus 23:12): ״למען ינוח שורך וחמורך״. ״כבס ביין לבושו״ alludes to the sanctity of wine on the Sabbath. And he said ״חכלילי עינים מיין״, from what we find regarding the Sabbath, that the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: a wide stride takes away one five-hundredth of the light of a person's eyes, and the Kiddush of the evening of Shabbat restores it."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {1 "Comment [1] contains multiple method-headers fused in sequence: it opens with 'By way of the Midrash' then transitions to 'And know the view of Onkelos' as a distinct discussion, followed by peshat-style analysis of the missing zayin; rendered each header as written without merging."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:21:45.347128972Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}